.TH WHY-SLOW 1 "September 2026" "why-slow 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
why-slow \- read-only Linux performance observations and cautious explanations
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B why-slow
.RI [ snapshot | sample | processes | summary ]
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B why-slow
collects selected local Linux performance observations without changing system
configuration. It distinguishes observations, indicators, context, unavailable
data and insufficient evidence. Correlation does not establish causation.
No telemetry, network access, automatic optimization or persistent report writing
is performed. Ordinary users can collect whatever the kernel permits them to read.
.SH MODES
.TP
.B snapshot
One reading. Gauges and cumulative counters are available; CPU percentages and
counter rates require a second reading and are not invented from boot totals.
.TP
.B sample
Three readings by default, with rates between adjacent readings.
.TP
.B processes
Two readings by default. Includes the same findings plus process rankings.
.TP
.B summary
Two readings by default, using the same evidence and finding model.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI "--format " format
text (default), json or markdown. JSON stdout is one complete document with a
samples array. Raw integer counters are decimal strings to preserve precision.
Diagnostics go to stderr.
.TP
.BI "--interval " seconds
Requested sampling interval, 0.05 through 60; default 1. Actual monotonic elapsed
time is used for rates. Short intervals amplify accounting and collection skew.
.TP
.BI "--samples " count
Total readings, including the baseline; 2 through 1000. Mutually exclusive with
.BR --duration .
.TP
.BI "--duration " seconds
Observe for up to one hour, with a final reading at or after the requested endpoint.
At most 1000 readings are allowed; increase the interval for long sessions.
Collection time and scheduler delay can extend wall time. This and other sampling
options require a sampling mode.
.TP
.BI "--timeout " seconds
Deadline per entire collection, 0.1 through 30 seconds; default 5. A timed-out
collector yields unavailable data, not zero values.
.TP
.BI "--top " count
Process rows in text/Markdown, 1 through 100; default 10. Rankings use sampled
CPU when available, otherwise RSS. JSON retains all collected process records.
.TP
.B --explain
Include selected raw observations and derived metrics in text/Markdown.
JSON always includes these values.
.TP
.BI "--fixture-root " directory
Read fixture proc and sys subdirectories. Fixture paths cannot contain symlinks.
Filesystem capacity is read from explicit fixture files; host mount paths are
not queried. Intended for reproducible tests.
.TP
.B --help
Show usage.
.TP
.B --version
Show version 1.0.0.
.SH INTERPRETATION
CPU execution percentages use deltas, exclude idle, iowait and steal from busy,
and do not double-count guest time. High load is not CPU percentage: load includes
runnable and certain uninterruptible entities. Iowait is not proof of disk latency.
.PP
MemAvailable is preferred to MemFree. Linux uses memory for caches. Swap occupancy
alone does not show active swapping; sampled swap traffic and memory PSI are
separate observations. Positive I/O counters do not demonstrate contention.
.PP
PSI measures resource-stalled time. Its averages are rolling history, not a
snapshot interval. Cgroup/namespace scope can differ from physical host scope.
CPU quota equivalents are shared upper bounds, not dedicated CPUs.
.PP
Filesystem capacity is distinct from latency and contention. Per-device I/O is
never totaled across potentially stacked devices. High temperature or reduced
frequency does not establish thermal throttling; only appropriate counter
increments support that observation. A top-ranked process is not blamed for the
user's slowdown. No combined health score is assigned.
.SH PRIVACY
Hostnames, usernames, network addresses, machine IDs, serial numbers, filesystem
UUIDs, mount paths, full command lines and environments are not reported.
Process names, PIDs and workload counters remain potentially identifying; review
reports before sharing. Untrusted terminal controls are sanitized.
.SH SIGNALS
SIGINT and SIGTERM end sampling and finish JSON where output remains writable.
Collection can take the remaining timeout plus up to 200 ms of child cleanup.
The supervisor may terminate only its own disposable collector on timeout; it
never signals analyzed processes. An uninterruptible kernel task cannot be
forced to exit immediately. A broken output pipe is handled without a crash dump.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
At least one reading had CPU, memory or process core data. Not a health verdict.
.TP
.B 1
A collector failed (sampling stops), or all readings lacked core data.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command line.
.TP
.B 3
Output or internal failure; the output document may be incomplete.
.TP
.B 130, 143
Interrupted by SIGINT or SIGTERM, respectively.
.TP
.B 141
Broken stdout pipe.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
why-slow snapshot
why-slow sample --interval 1 --samples 6
why-slow sample --duration 30 --interval 2 --format json
why-slow processes --interval 2 --top 15
why-slow summary --format markdown
.fi
.SH LIMITATIONS
Reads are non-atomic. Missing kernel features, permissions and container scope
limit coverage. Threads are not separately scanned. Cgroup v1 is not interpreted.
Application locks, network latency, transient stalls between samples and physical
hardware behavior may not be diagnosed. No causal proof of slowness is promised.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR proc (5),
.BR statvfs (3)
